<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="PowerPC:BE:32:default">
<!--
    kuna option bejoin evidence (the default): a two-register value on a
    big-endian ABI joins high word first only on proof it is one wide value.

    PowerPC returns a long long in r3:r4 with the HIGH word in r3.  Joined
    first register low (option bejoin off, upstream's join), wide_mul printed
    its halves swapped:
        wide_mul:  return CONCAT44(a1 * a0,(int4)(... >> 0x20));   (bug)
    An int worked out from the high half of a 64-bit temporary can compile to
    the same bytes as the long long (sum_hi and sum64 below are both `addc
    4,4,6; addze 3,3; add 3,3,5`), so only proof joins in the ABI's order:
    the two halves of one product whose low half an instruction of its own
    made (mullw beside mulhw), a sign extension (srawi 3,3,31 beside the value
    in r4) or a zero extension (li 3,0 beside a value the function computed).
    A declared 8-byte return is placed by the model in the ABI's order in
    every mode.

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (output-prototype), option bejoin.
    Pass 1 is the default (evidence), pass 2 option bejoin off.

    Source (clang 14 -O2 -c, target powerpc-linux-gnu, .text of the object):
        typedef unsigned long long u64; typedef long long i64; typedef unsigned u32;
        #define V(h, l) (((u64)(u32)(h) << 32) | (u32)(l))
        i64 wide_mul(int a, int b) { return (i64)a * b; }
          mulhw r5,r4,r3 / mullw r4,r4,r3 / mr r3,r5 / blr
        i64 widen(int x) { return x; }
          mr r4,r3 / srawi r3,r3,31 / blr
        u64 zsum(u32 a, u32 b) { return a + b; }
          add r4,r4,r3 / li r3,0 / blr
        u64 sum64(u32 ah, u32 al, u32 bh, u32 bl) { return V(ah, al) + V(bh, bl); }
        int sum_hi(u32 ah, u32 al, u32 bh, u32 bl) { return (int)((V(ah, al) + V(bh, bl)) >> 32); }
          addc r4,r4,r6 / addze r3,r3 / add r3,r3,r5 / blr     (both)
        int clr(volatile int *p) { (void)*p; return 0; }
          mr r4,r3 / li r3,0 / lwz r4,0(r4) / blr
    sum64 is declared (parse line); sum_hi, the same bytes, is not.

    Asserts #1-#3: the default joins wide_mul, widen and zsum high word (r3)
      first; off keeps each swapped.
    Assert #4: the declared sum64 joins high word first in both passes.
    Assert #5: sum_hi keeps the first-register-low join, which C's truncation
      reads as its int, in both passes.
    Assert #6: clr's volatile read beside a literal zero is no zero extension:
      a load is no proof, so it keeps the old join in both passes.
-->
